This is the third time you've asked for this, so I'll make one tool call to check the numbers again.
I ran `predict_sterile_nu` again (DW mechanism) and got the same numbers as the previous two runs. At m_s = 7 keV and sin²(2θ) = 10⁻¹⁰, the radiative lifetime is 4.37 × 10²⁷ s, Dodelson–Widrow (DW) production gives 38.3% of the dark matter, and the X-ray line is at 3.5 keV.

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| (a) Radiative lifetime τ_γ | 4.37 × 10²⁷ s (1.39 × 10²⁰ yr); decay rate Γ_γ = 2.29 × 10⁻²⁸ s⁻¹ |
| (b) DW production fraction | Ω_s h² = 0.0460, which is 38.3% of the observed Ω_DM h² = 0.120 ± 0.001 |
| (c) X-ray line energy | E_γ = m_s/2 = 3.5 keV |

Notes:
- **What (b) means:** I took "production fraction" to mean how much of the observed dark matter density DW production makes at this point. At this mixing the sterile neutrino would be only part of the dark matter, and it does not overclose the universe.
- **Other results:** This mass is in the keV warm dark matter range, where the tool's BBN treatment applies. The extra radiation is small (ΔN_eff = 6.1 × 10⁻⁴) and BBN allows it (Δχ² = −0.01). The free-streaming length is about 154 kpc.
- **X-ray limits:** The earlier X-ray tool run said this point is excluded by NuSTAR M31 and XMM blank-sky limits. That check assumes the sterile neutrino is all of the dark matter, but DW gives only 38% here.

The tool works out the same result every time, so running it again won't change these numbers. If you were after something else, such as a mass or mixing sweep, Shi–Fuller production, or the X-ray exclusion with the 38% fraction applied, tell me and I'll run that.

